INT. PAWN SHOP BACK OFFICE - NIGHT

A desk lamp. A safe with its door ajar. Boxes of watches, wedding rings, guitars.

SOLOMON "SOL" ADEYEMI-BRANDT, 60s, cardigan, reading glasses, counts bills with a licked thumb. Behind him on a shelf, a clock.

The door opens a crack. RENATA "REN" PAPADOPOULOS-ESEZOBOR, 30s, in a hooded sweatshirt, slips in, keeping one hand in the pocket.

SOL
We're closed.

REN
Door was open.

SOL
Doors do that.

REN
You Sol?

SOL
Depends.

REN
Gabe sent me.

SOL
Gabe's in Tucson.

REN
Gabe's not.

Sol stops counting. Folds the bills. Puts them in a drawer, slowly. Closes the drawer with his knee.

SOL
Sit.

REN
I'll stand.

SOL
Suit yourself.

She stays by the door. He picks up a tea mug, sips, sets it down. The clock ticks.

REN
He says you owe him.

SOL
Everybody owes somebody.

REN
Eight thousand.

SOL
I've got six.

REN
He said eight.

SOL
He said a lot of things.

Ren takes a step. The floor creaks. Sol's hand slides from the desk toward the lamp's base.

SOL (CONT'D)
That's close enough.

REN
You scared?

SOL
I'm seventy. Everything scares me.

REN
You said sixty.

SOL
Sixty-nine.

He laughs. She doesn't. Her hand comes out of the pocket, empty, then goes back in.

SOL (CONT'D)
What's in there?

REN
Gloves.

SOL
It's July.

REN
It's a long night.

Sol rises. Slowly. His chair rolls back, bumps the safe. Ren darts forward, he swings the lamp, she ducks, they collide against the desk, and the lamp crashes to the floor in a spray of glass. Darkness.

A grunt. A thud. Something metal skitters.

The lamp's bulb sputters and relights, flickering. Sol sits on the floor with his back against the safe, breathing hard. Ren crouches over him, gloves on, a rubber hammer in her fist.

SOL (CONT'D)
(wheezing)
Gloves.

REN
Told you.

She lowers the hammer. Sets it on the carpet. Sol pats his chest.

SOL
Take the six.

REN
Gabe will want eight.

SOL
Then tell him I'm dead.

REN
He'll check.

SOL
Let him.

She stands. Opens the drawer, takes the bills. Counts. Doesn't count.

REN
You said sixty.

SOL
I said sixty-nine.

She almost smiles. Pockets the cash and goes. The door clicks. Sol leans his head against the safe, listens to the clock, and shuts his eyes.
